(3443401, 470338, 23430, '<td valign="top">Really good shot, very different.</td>', 'Really good shot, very different.', '2007-03-08 13:46:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(3443413, 476480, 52336, '<td valign="top">I really love the regularity of the composition (all the lines and geometry) offset by the beautiful shingling above and the unique drapes, and of course the central human figure.  I agree that it has a charming European quality, and is lovely to look at for a while.</td>', 'I really love the regularity of the composition (all the lines and geometry) offset by the beautiful shingling above and the unique drapes, and of course the central human figure.  I agree that it has a charming European quality, and is lovely to look at for a while.', '2007-03-08 13:49:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(3443417, 476178, 5792, '<td valign="top">Positives:<br/>There are emotional aspects of this image that are appealing. The boy in front of the fire begs the viewer to speculate what the child is experiencing and thinking. Placing the subject between the brightest flame and photographer works well.<br/> <br/>Technicals:<br/>Sharpness is not bad, but could be better.<br/> <br/>A problem with low light situations is that the image tends to be overly yellow(as is likely in your case) or red(as common with people images) because of white balance issues under those conditions. If you have to overcome those issue then you may already have lost the battle for a great photograph regardless of content.<br/> <br/>You did the best you could under those conditions but really stood no change to overcome that.<br/> <br/>In its own way the use of yellow is fine, but the lack of other color detail gives it a superficial, overprocessed appearance.<br/> <br/>The challenge:<br/>Again, the assumption of voters for a free study is images of the highest technical quality. When disappointed they punish the images with a low score. That is not to say the image deserves the lower score, it is just the way DPC voters think.</td>', 'Positives:There are emotional aspects of this image that are appealing. The boy in front of the fire begs the viewer to speculate what the child is experiencing and thinking. Placing the subject between the brightest flame and photographer works well. Technicals:Sharpness is not bad, but could be better. A problem with low light situations is that the image tends to be overly yellow(as is likely in your case) or red(as common with people images) because of white balance issues under those conditions. If you have to overcome those issue then you may already have lost the battle for a great photograph regardless of content. You did the best you could under those conditions but really stood no change to overcome that. In its own way the use of yellow is fine, but the lack of other color detail gives it a superficial, overprocessed appearance. The challenge:Again, the assumption of voters for a free study is images of the highest technical quality. When disappointed they punish the images with a low score. That is not to say the image deserves the lower score, it is just the way DPC voters think.', '2007-03-08 13:49:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(3443475, 470338, 37961, '<td valign="top">It truly is beautiful.</td>', 'It truly is beautiful.', '2007-03-08 14:06:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(3443535, 476480, 42100, '<td valign="top">I didn''t get a chance to comment on this during the challenge, so I''m so glad it was brought back to my attention.  I just wanted to say how much I enjoyed this photograph, not as much for aesthetic reasons as sentimental ones.  <br/> <br/>It reminds me so much from when I was I child and we would go to visit my great-grandmother in Germany.  She would always be at her street-front window chatting with one neighbor or another when we arrived.  Before she got too old, she would bake big sheets of potato cakes and pass them out to everybody.  She was a real character...thanks for the flashback!</td>', 'I didn''t get a chance to comment on this during the challenge, so I''m so glad it was brought back to my attention.  I just wanted to say how much I enjoyed this photograph, not as much for aesthetic reasons as sentimental ones.   It reminds me so much from when I was I child and we would go to visit my great-grandmother in Germany.  She would always be at her street-front window chatting with one neighbor or another when we arrived.  Before she got too old, she would bake big sheets of potato cakes and pass them out to everybody.  She was a real character...thanks for the flashback!', '2007-03-08 14:29:18.000000', NULL, 0),
