(3444635, 473095, 93, '<td valign="top">Just a note:<br/> <br/>When you do processing/painting/spot work on a black background, it''s a good idea to turn the gamma on your monitor way up, so that you can get as even a paint-job as possible to the widest range of monitors out there.<br/> <br/>On my monitor, I can easily see where you''ve darkened the upper left corner and all around the front of your model.  I don''t know if this was to clone something out, or whatever, but the job is very noticeable.  If you pump the gamma up, you can make sure that this work is evened out through the whole of the photograph, and leaves you less prone to those of us with brighter monitors.   Granted, there aren''t a lot of us, but I find that it helps even to the mid-range much of the time.<br/> <br/>(oops.. sorry, I see now that you touched on this yourself. You can remember it for the future though..  gamma gamma gamma! :))<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-03-08 21:03:12.</i></td>', 'Just a note: When you do processing/painting/spot work on a black background, it''s a good idea to turn the gamma on your monitor way up, so that you can get as even a paint-job as possible to the widest range of monitors out there. On my monitor, I can easily see where you''ve darkened the upper left corner and all around the front of your model.  I don''t know if this was to clone something out, or whatever, but the job is very noticeable.  If you pump the gamma up, you can make sure that this work is evened out through the whole of the photograph, and leaves you less prone to those of us with brighter monitors.   Granted, there aren''t a lot of us, but I find that it helps even to the mid-range much of the time. (oops.. sorry, I see now that you touched on this yourself. You can remember it for the future though..  gamma gamma gamma! :))', '2007-03-08 21:01:15.000000', '2007-03-08 21:03:12.000000', 0),
