(3447143, 477190, 70828, '<td valign="top">I have to add that there is something very deep about this image that hurts me deeply ... I guess that means it is extremely moving and doing what you intended ... I just thought that I would come out and say that ... which I did not before ... because the feeling was so profound that I felt it should be mentioned ...</td>', 'I have to add that there is something very deep about this image that hurts me deeply ... I guess that means it is extremely moving and doing what you intended ... I just thought that I would come out and say that ... which I did not before ... because the feeling was so profound that I felt it should be mentioned ...', '2007-03-09 17:59:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(3447148, 477021, 14591, '<td valign="top">Definitely a photoshop deal, and not a very good one. There are several aspects that give it away, but the number of stars you put in are ridiculous. When you have an exposure that was long enough to capture this many stars (and apparently a couple of galaxies and nebulas also) you would have to have the darkest sky imaginable. An aurora at the same time would bleed everything out. That''s why you usually see very few stars, because the aurora can outshine most of them. The moon can be out at the same time, but the aurora is always on the northern horizon so the moon isn''t in the shot. Even so, anything close to a full moon makes it very difficult for the aurora to shine through.<br/> <br/>The uniformity and distribution of stars is also a dead giveaway. You may have used an actual shot of stars as your background, but it was taken through a telescope of a much smaller section of sky.<br/> <br/>Aurora is brilliant, but paleeeeze show us something new.  ughhhh<br/>playahata<br/>kool kap''n kablinki</td>', 'Definitely a photoshop deal, and not a very good one. There are several aspects that give it away, but the number of stars you put in are ridiculous. When you have an exposure that was long enough to capture this many stars (and apparently a couple of galaxies and nebulas also) you would have to have the darkest sky imaginable. An aurora at the same time would bleed everything out. That''s why you usually see very few stars, because the aurora can outshine most of them. The moon can be out at the same time, but the aurora is always on the northern horizon so the moon isn''t in the shot. Even so, anything close to a full moon makes it very difficult for the aurora to shine through. The uniformity and distribution of stars is also a dead giveaway. You may have used an actual shot of stars as your background, but it was taken through a telescope of a much smaller section of sky. Aurora is brilliant, but paleeeeze show us something new.  ughhhhplayahatakool kap''n kablinki', '2007-03-09 18:02:04.000000', NULL, 0),
